      summaryEn: "A wave of adaptations of Austen, Dickens and other classics has put literary titles back on screens. The Economist argues that familiar stories reduce commercial uncertainty yet still demand interpretation: every version chooses which characters, histories and moral emphases to foreground. Industry data show strong demand for book-based productions, while publishers report sales gains for some originals. That does not disprove a broader decline in leisure reading; instead it shows that screen and page can sometimes reinforce each other.",
