      backgroundEn: "The article asks whether the boom signals cultural exhaustion or a durable form of storytelling. It considers new versions of Sense and Sensibility and East of Eden alongside earlier screen interpretations. The New Yorker’s profile of filmmaker Paweł Pawlikowski elsewhere in this week’s materials supplies a different angle on how film reworks European memory, though it is not the same adaptation trend.",
      backgroundZh: "文章追问改编热是文化创意衰竭，还是故事传播的持久方式，并比较《理智与情感》《伊甸之东》的新旧版本。本周《纽约客》对导演 Paweł Pawlikowski 的人物特写则从欧洲记忆如何被电影重塑提供另一角度，但并非同一改编潮流。",
      argumentEn: ["Known books bring audiences and lower marketing risk for producers.", "A strong adaptation is not mere fidelity: it reveals a contemporary reading of the source.", "Successful screen versions can send some viewers back to books, but isolated sales spikes should not be mistaken for a general reading renaissance."],
      argumentZh: ["名著自带观众，降低制作方营销风险。", "优秀改编不只是忠于原著，而是呈现当代对原作的理解。", "成功影视版能带动部分观众读书，但个别销量激增不等于全民阅读复兴。"],
      evidenceEn: "The article cites Parrot Analytics demand comparisons and publisher or BookScan sales figures, plus interviews with screenwriters and publishing professionals. These show that particular adaptations and books performed well. They do not establish that adaptations caused every sale, or that serious reading is rising overall.",
